    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im having an issue with our sublimation heat press setup in our facility. the colors arent transferring deep enough into the polyester fabric, it looks really faded and washed out even when we run it through twice. what should i be checking for regarding temp and pressure settings?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Faded colors on sublimation are almost always a sign that your heat press isn't properly engaging with the fabric, which is usually a problem with temperature or pressure. You need to ensure your heat press is hitting the exact temperature required for the ink to properly bond with the polyester, and the pressure needs to be sufficient to force the dye into the fibers. If you're running it twice and it still looks washed out, it's likely the heat or pressure is too low, or perhaps your sublimation paper is old or has been exposed to too much light.\n\nFor the best results, start by verifying your temperature setting against the specific polyester fabric you are using, as different blends require different heat levels. A common starting point for standard polyester is around 385°F (195°C), but if the fabric is thicker, you might need to increase it slightly. Pressure is equally important; you need enough force to make the transfer material make full, intimate contact with the fabric. If you're using a flat press, try to apply consistent, even pressure across the entire area. If you're using a clamshell, make sure the platen is fully closing and sealing around the material.\n\nIt's",
      "source": "target",
